  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Paolo Fassa is an Italian industrialist who expanded his family’s lime business into Fassa Bortolo, a manufacturer of plasters, paints and other building materials. Born in Spresiano near Treviso, he took ownership after buying out his brothers and developed production beyond Italy. He later became honorary president of the family-controlled group.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "University chronology, company history and current governance agree."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Paolo Fassa is an Italian industrialist who expanded his family’s lime business into Fassa Bortolo, a manufacturer of plasters, paints and other building materials. Born in Spresiano near Treviso, he took ownership after buying out his brothers and developed production beyond Italy. He later became honorary president of the family-controlled group.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "When his mother asked him to join the family business, Paolo Fassa was initially unsure about the opportunity. Sitting at his new desk, he used a fountain pen to scratch the date into a paperweight. He was beginning work alongside his father in sales and production, with ambitions to modernise the company.\n\nOld industrial buildings later became part of the business’s working environment. Fassa Bortolo restored the nineteenth-century Lazzaris timber complex in Spresiano for its headquarters, then converted an old spinning mill into a training centre. These projects gave former manufacturing sites new uses for office staff, construction professionals, meetings and technical learning.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "First-person account supplies the desk episode; university chronology supplies both adaptive-use projects."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 103,
    "html": "<p>When his mother asked him to join the family business, Paolo Fassa was initially unsure about the opportunity. Sitting at his new desk, he used a fountain pen to scratch the date into a paperweight. He was beginning work alongside his father in sales and production, with ambitions to modernise the company.</p>\r\n<p>Old industrial buildings later became part of the business’s working environment. Fassa Bortolo restored the nineteenth-century Lazzaris timber complex in Spresiano for its headquarters, then converted an old spinning mill into a training centre. These projects gave former manufacturing sites new uses for office staff, construction professionals, meetings and technical learning.</p>\r\n"
  },
